<section id="scrollSection">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2><span class="num">03</span>Back to where you were</h2>
        <p class="lede">
            The engine swaps content in place, so the browser cannot restore a history entry's scroll
            position by itself. <code>config.restoreScroll</code> (on by default) has the engine keep it:
            each entry it owns stores a <code>scrollY</code> in its state, saved as you scroll and at the
            moment a link is clicked. Back, forward and reload scroll back to it once the entry's content
            is mounted.
        </p>
        <div class="release-grid">
            <div>
                <div class="panel">
                    <div class="panel-h"><span>try it</span></div>
                    <div class="panel-b">
                        <ol class="steps">
                            <li>Scroll this page so this section sits somewhere you will recognise.</li>
                            <li>Press <b>Open User 7 from here</b>, or use the nav above. The entry you leave
                                keeps its position.</li>
                            <li>Press the browser's <b>Back</b>: the home route is mounted again and the page
                                returns to the same spot. <b>Forward</b> does the same for User 7.</li>
                            <li><b>Reload</b>: the entry's state survives it, so you land where you were.</li>
                        </ol>
                        <div class="row" style="margin-top:14px">
                            <button class="primary" id="btnLeaveHere">Open User 7 from here</button>
                            <label class="lbl" style="margin:0; display:flex; align-items:center; gap:8px; cursor:pointer">
                                <input type="checkbox" id="restoreToggle" checked> config.restoreScroll
                            </label>
                        </div>
                        <p class="note" style="margin:10px 0 0">The setting is applied at <code>initialize()</code>, so
                            toggling it reloads the page with <code>?restoreScroll=0</code>. Off, Back lands wherever the
                            previous route left the page.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="panel">
                    <div class="panel-h"><span>in your app</span></div>
                    <pre class="snip">// on by default; read at initialize()
spa.config.restoreScroll = true;

// a page that places scroll itself on back/forward:
spa.config.restoreScroll = false;

// the engine never scrolls on a link click: open the new
// route at the top (or a #section) in your click callback
window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: "instant" });</pre>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div>
                <div class="panel">
                    <div class="panel-h"><span>scroll state, live</span></div>
                    <div class="panel-b">
                        <table id="scrollTable">
                            <thead><tr><th>reading</th><th>value</th><th>what it is</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>window.scrollY</td><td id="ssNow">0</td><td>where the page is now</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>history.state.scrollY</td><td id="ssSaved">unset</td><td>what the engine saved for this entry; follows the page about 100ms after scrolling stops</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>history.length</td><td id="ssLength">1</td><td>one entry per route visited; the page you opened adds no second one</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>config.restoreScroll</td><td id="ssEnabled">true</td><td>read once, at <code>initialize()</code></td></tr>
                                <tr id="ssLastRow"><td>last restore</td><td id="ssLast">none yet</td><td id="ssLastNote">press Back or Forward, or reload</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p class="note" style="margin-top:12px">
                            The User 8 link uses <code>data-add-to-history="false"</code>: it pushes no entry,
                            so it saves no position either, and Back skips over it.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

